{"info": {"author": null, "author_email": "Herv\u00e9 BREDIN <herve@pyannote.ai>", "bugtrack_url": null, "classifiers": [], "description": "![pyannote.metrics](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pyannote/pyannote-metrics/main/.github/social-preview.png)\n\n> a toolkit for reproducible evaluation, diagnostic, and error analysis of speaker diarization systems\n\nAn overview of `pyannote.metrics` is available as an [InterSpeech 2017 paper](https://github.com/pyannote/pyannote-metrics/blob/main/doc/pyannote-metrics.pdf): it is recommended to read it first, to quickly get an idea whether this tool is for you.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\n$ pip install pyannote.metrics\n```\n\n## Documentation\n\nThe documentation is available at [http://pyannote.github.io/pyannote-metrics](http://pyannote.github.io/pyannote-metrics).\n\nSample notebooks are available [here](http://nbviewer.ipython.org/github/pyannote/pyannote-metrics/blob/main/notebooks/index.ipynb).\n\n## Citation\n\nIf you use `pyannote.metrics` in your research, please use the following citation:\n\n```bibtex\n@inproceedings{pyannote.metrics,\n  author = {Herv\\'e Bredin},\n  title = {{pyannote.metrics: a toolkit for reproducible evaluation, diagnostic, and error analysis of speaker diarization systems}},\n  booktitle = {{Interspeech 2017, 18th Annual Conference of the International Speech Communication Association}},\n  year = {2017},\n  month = {August},\n  address = {Stockholm, Sweden},\n  url = {http://pyannote.github.io/pyannote-metrics},\n}\n```\n", "description_content_type": "text/markdown", "docs_url": "https://pythonhosted.org/pyannote-metrics/", "download_url": null, "downloads": {"last_day": -1, "last_month": -1, "last_week": -1}, "dynamic": null, "home_page": null, "keywords": null, "license": null, "license_expression": null, "license_files": ["LICENSE"], "maintainer": null, "maintainer_email": null, "name": "pyannote-metrics", "package_url": "https://pypi.org/project/pyannote-metrics/", "platform": nul